From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: finer GPU back-pressure polling on macOS

NOTE: this patch could not be measured where it was written (no macOS
build, no trace) and is the least certain of the gp series. It rests on
reading the code only. Treat the expected gain as a hypothesis to A/B.

Stock behaviour
---------------
Two waits on the GPU main thread (CrGpuMain) apply back-pressure to a WebGL
canvas on macOS. Both only wait when the previous frame's GPU work has not
finished yet, and both have a 1-2 ms granularity.

1. Command buffer deschedule.
   third_party/blink/renderer/platform/graphics/gpu/drawing_buffer.cc,
   DrawingBuffer::FinishPrepareTransferableResourceGpu(), under
   IS_MAC || IS_ANDROID, ends every exported frame with
     gl_->DescheduleUntilFinishedCHROMIUM();
   GLES2DecoderPassthroughImpl::DoDescheduleUntilFinishedCHROMIUM()
   (gles2_cmd_decoder_passthrough_doers.cc) creates a gl::GLFence, keeps at
   most two in deschedule_until_finished_fences_, and if the older one (the
   previous frame) has not completed calls client()->OnDescheduleUntilFinished()
   and returns error::kDeferLaterCommands. CommandBufferStub then does
   command_buffer_->SetScheduled(false). The only thing that ever looks at
   the fence again is the stub's timer:
   gpu/ipc/service/command_buffer_stub.cc
     const int64_t kHandleMoreWorkPeriodMs = 2;      // first poll
     const int64_t kHandleMoreWorkPeriodBusyMs = 1;  // every poll after
   ~ScopedContextOperation() -> ScheduleDelayedWork(2 ms) -> PollWork() ->
   PerformWork() -> decoder->PerformPollingWork() ->
   ProcessDescheduleUntilFinished() (HasCompleted() on the fence, then
   OnRescheduleAfterFinished()) -> ScheduleDelayedWork(1 ms) and so on.
   So once descheduled, the WebGL context resumes no earlier than 2 ms later
   and then on a 1 ms grid.
   The same two constants also pace, on every platform, the polling of
   pending queries, idle work (GPU tracer, async ReadPixels) and WebGPU's
   Dawn polling (WebGPUDecoderImpl::HasPollingWork). None of that is touched.

2. Presenter fence wait.
   ui/accelerated_widget_mac/ca_layer_tree_coordinator.mm,
   CALayerTreeCoordinator::ApplyBackpressure(), called from
   ImageTransportSurfaceOverlayMacEGL::CommitPresentedFrameToCA() for every
   presented compositor frame, waits for the MTLSharedEvent fences of the
   previously committed frame's overlay IOSurfaces (for the WebGL canvas:
   the event ANGLE/Metal records when the context ends its write access, see
   IOSurfaceImageBacking) in a loop around
     base::PlatformThread::Sleep(base::Milliseconds(1));
   This blocks the GPU main thread itself, i.e. also WebGL command decoding
   for the next frame.

Both are on the per-frame path with --disable-gpu-vsync
--disable-frame-rate-limit: neither flag is consulted on these paths, and
with VSyncAlignedPresentation off (the default) Present() normally commits
to CoreAnimation immediately, through ApplyBackpressure() (frames flagged
is_handling_interaction are held until the display link callback, which
takes the same path). When the GPU is the
limiting side at several hundred FPS, a wait that should last a fraction of
a millisecond is rounded up to 1-2 ms, which is of the order of a frame.

Change
------
macOS only (every changed line is inside BUILDFLAG(IS_MAC)), behind
KccMacFineGpuBackpressure:

- CommandBufferStub::ScheduleDelayedWork(): when the command buffer is
  unscheduled - which in CommandBufferStub happens only between
  OnDescheduleUntilFinished() and OnRescheduleAfterFinished() - the
  requested delay is scaled so that the 1 ms repeat delay becomes poll_us
  and the 2 ms first delay becomes 2 * poll_us. The upstream comment on the
  constants explains that the first poll is deliberately longer ("or else
  the timing of the fences could allow a pattern of alternating fast and
  slow frames"), so the 2:1 shape is kept rather than flattened. Every
  other use of the constants (command buffer scheduled) is unchanged.
- CALayerTreeCoordinator::ApplyBackpressure(): the sleep between fence
  checks is poll_us instead of 1 ms.

The wait conditions themselves are untouched: back-pressure is exactly as
strong as before, it is only noticed sooner.

Because one feature is read from two components (gpu/ipc/service and
ui/accelerated_widget_mac) and base::Feature must have a single definition
per name, the feature and its param live in the anonymous namespace of
ui/gl/gl_features.cc and both users call the exported accessor
features::KccMacFineGpuBackpressurePollPeriod() (ui/gl/gl_features.h, IS_MAC
only), which returns zero when the feature is off. Both components already
depend on //ui/gl.

Gating / A-B
------------
base::Feature KccMacFineGpuBackpressure, disabled by default.
FeatureParam poll_us (int, microseconds, default 250, clamped to
100..2000); feature state and param are read once. poll_us/1000 reproduces
the stock timing with the feature on; values above 1000 are slower than
stock.
  --enable-features=KccMacFineGpuBackpressure
  --enable-features=KccMacFineGpuBackpressure:poll_us/500
With the feature off the accessor returns zero and both sites use the stock
delays.
Measure on macOS arm64, uncapped, in a GPU-heavy scene (the patch does
nothing when the GPU keeps up): FPS and 1% lows; in a trace (categories
gpu, cc) the duration of "GLES2DecoderPassthroughImpl::DescheduleUntilFinished"
async slices, of "CALayerTreeCoordinator::ApplyBackpressure" and the
Gpu.Mac.BackpressureUs histogram, and the count of
"CommandBufferStub::PerformWork". If the two waits never show up in a stock
trace of the game, this patch is not worth carrying.

Risks / not verified
--------------------
- Nothing was compiled; macOS-only code paths, including an Objective-C++
  file.
- Timer precision: the deschedule poll is a base::DeadlineTimer (kPrecise)
  on the GPU main thread's NS run loop pump, i.e. a CFRunLoopTimer with zero
  tolerance; the presenter wait is nanosleep. Whether macOS actually
  delivers ~250 us wake-ups there was not measured.
- More wake-ups while GPU-bound: up to 1/poll_us PollWork tasks per second
  per descheduled context, each doing MakeCurrent + query/readback/fence
  checks, instead of 1000/s. With heavy GPU-bound content at low FPS this is
  extra GPU-main-thread CPU for no benefit.
- The upstream warning about alternating fast and slow frames was written
  for the 2 ms first-poll delay; whether 2 * poll_us is still enough to
  avoid that pattern is unknown. If frame times start alternating, raise
  poll_us.
- TimeDelta * double is used for the scaling (as upstream does elsewhere).

